# SteamHangar vault-dns -- production dnsmasq config TEMPLATE (Phase 1, WP 1.8).
#
# This is a TEMPLATE, not a ready-to-use config: every ${VAR} placeholder
# below is substituted by `envsubst` when the vault-dns container starts.
# Wiring that substitution into an actual container entrypoint/Compose
# service is WP 1.9's job -- this work package defines the file and the
# placeholder CONTRACT only. See "Placeholder contract" in dns/README.md
# for the full variable list, required defaults, and who is responsible
# for supplying them (WP 1.9's entrypoint, not this file).
#
# Derives directly from the proven pattern in
# poc/linux-client-test/scenario-b.sh (Phase 0, WP 0.6 + follow-up fix),
# the first real evidence the vault-dns approach works for Linux/Steam
# Deck-class clients, not just Windows. Two differences from that
# throwaway WSL2 test instance, both because this file targets a
# long-running container instead of a one-off test harness:
#   - upstream forwarders and the cache IP are configurable here, not
#     hardcoded literals
#   - listens on all interfaces (0.0.0.0), not just 127.0.0.1 -- a
#     container's loopback isn't reachable from the LAN. WP 1.9's Compose
#     file is what actually restricts *external* exposure (which host
#     interface/port gets published); this file only controls what the
#     daemon does *inside* the container.
#
# Validated by dns/tests/test-dnsmasq-config.ps1 (`dnsmasq --test`, plus an
# optional live A/AAAA functional check) against dnsmasq 2.92.

# --- AAAA / IPv6-bypass guard (ADR-0001 req 6) -- READ THIS FIRST -----------
# THE SINGLE MOST IMPORTANT LINE PAIR IN THIS FILE.
#
# Verified live on dnsmasq 2.92 during Phase 0 (poc/linux-client-test,
# WP 0.6 follow-up fix, see docs/adr/0001-proxy-store-feasibility.md
# requirement 6): `address=/DOMAIN/IP` alone only intercepts the DNS
# record TYPE it was given a literal for -- here an IPv4 address, so only
# A queries are answered locally. Every OTHER query type for the same
# name, including AAAA, is FORWARDED UPSTREAM by modern dnsmasq and comes
# back as Valve's real, routable IPv6 address. An IPv6-capable client
# (increasingly the common case -- "happy eyeballs" prefers AAAA when
# present) would then connect directly to the real Steam CDN over IPv6,
# silently bypassing this cache entirely -- no error, no log evidence on
# this side, just a client that mysteriously never shows up as a cache
# hit (this is exactly the failure mode requirement A12/bypass-detection
# has to assume can happen).
#
# The fix, and it is a REQUIRED pairing, not optional hardening:
# `local=/DOMAIN/` makes dnsmasq authoritative for the whole zone -- it
# answers from its own data for every RR type and never forwards zone
# queries upstream at all. Paired with address= (which supplies the
# concrete A answer), this makes AAAA -- and any other RR type for the
# zone -- resolve to NODATA (NOERROR, ANSWER: 0) locally instead of
# leaking upstream.
#
# Confirmed live during Phase 0: with a bare address= line, `dig AAAA
# cache2-ams1.steamcontent.com` returned Valve's real routable
# 2a01:bc80:... address. With local=/steamcontent.com/ added alongside
# it, the same query returns clean NODATA. docs/PROJECT_PLAN.md
# originally claimed "address= returns NODATA for AAAA" -- that claim was
# outdated and has been corrected there and in ADR-0001. Do not remove or
# "simplify" the local= line below; it is the entire reason this
# container exists instead of a plain `address=` one-liner.
address=/steamcontent.com/${CACHE_IP}
local=/steamcontent.com/

# --- Upstream forwarders -----------------------------------------------------
# no-resolv: never read /etc/resolv.conf for upstream servers. Inside a
# container, that file is typically managed by the container runtime and
# can point at a resolver that itself forwards back into this container's
# own Docker network -- relying on it risks a resolution loop, the same
# category of problem poc/linux-client-test/scenario-b.sh had to guard
# against on the WSL2 side, and that core/nginx/nginx.conf's own
# `resolver` directive had to guard against for vault-core (see
# core/README.md's loop-safety note). The explicit server= lines below
# are the ONLY upstream this daemon will ever use.
#
# ${UPSTREAM_DNS_1} / ${UPSTREAM_DNS_2}: substituted at container start.
# Documented defaults (supplied by WP 1.9's entrypoint/.env, NOT by this
# template -- see dns/README.md "Placeholder contract"): 1.1.1.1
# (Cloudflare) and 8.8.8.8 (Google), the same pair
# poc/linux-client-test/scenario-b.sh used successfully in Phase 0.
no-resolv
server=${UPSTREAM_DNS_1}
server=${UPSTREAM_DNS_2}

# --- Forwarding hygiene: deliberately ABSENT options -------------------------
# domain-needed and bogus-priv are standard dnsmasq hardening for a resolver
# that forwards to PUBLIC upstreams, and they are deliberately NOT set here
# (pre-freeze review P4 added them, review S4 removed them). The documented
# "point the upstream DNS at your router" mode (deploy/.env.example) makes
# the router the upstream, and the router is what answers the LAN's own
# names: domain-needed would answer bare names ("nas", "printer") locally
# with NXDOMAIN instead of forwarding them to the router, and bogus-priv
# would answer every LAN reverse lookup (192.168.x.x, 10.x.x.x, ...) with
# NXDOMAIN instead of the router's PTR record. Both silently break name
# resolution that works without vault-dns in the path.
# stop-dns-rebind is absent too: this resolver's entire purpose is to answer
# a public name (steamcontent.com) with a private address -- rebind
# protection would refuse exactly that answer.

# --- Listen configuration (container-appropriate) ---------------------------
# 0.0.0.0: unlike scenario-b.sh's 127.0.0.1-only WSL2 test instance, this
# daemon runs inside a container and must be reachable from LAN clients
# (or from the router's DHCP-assigned DNS server, once WP 1.9's Compose
# file publishes port 53 to a host interface). bind-interfaces makes
# dnsmasq bind directly to the addresses in listen-address (instead of
# the wildcard socket + per-request source-address filtering dnsmasq
# otherwise uses), which is required for listen-address to take effect
# predictably in a container's restricted network namespace.
#
# Exposure is NOT controlled here: this only governs what the daemon does
# INSIDE the container. WP 1.9's Compose file (port publishing / network
# mode) is what restricts which host interfaces actually reach it --
# scope boundary, not an oversight of this file.
#
# *** OPEN-RESOLVER WARNING -- BINDING REQUIREMENT FOR WP 1.9 ***
# This is a recursive/forwarding DNS server with NO source-address ACL
# (dnsmasq's --no-resolv + server= above define WHERE it forwards, not WHO
# is allowed to ask it to). Publishing this container's port 53 on 0.0.0.0
# or any WAN-facing interface turns it into a public open resolver -- a
# well-known abuse vector (DNS amplification/reflection attacks against
# third parties, using this homelab's bandwidth). WP 1.9's Compose file
# MUST publish port 53 on a specific LAN-only host IP/interface, e.g.
# `"192.168.1.50:53:53/udp"` (and the matching /tcp line), NEVER a bare
# `"53:53/udp"` (which Docker maps to 0.0.0.0 on the host, i.e. every
# interface including any WAN one) -- this is a hard requirement, not a
# style preference, and must be called out explicitly in WP 1.9's own
# compose/README so it isn't silently relaxed later.
#
# Caveat for whoever wires WP 1.9: in a bridge-network container, incoming
# connections are NAT'd (DNAT) to the container's internal 0.0.0.0:53, but
# the original client source IP is preserved end-to-end (DNAT rewrites the
# destination, not the source) -- dnsmasq's own listen-address/
# bind-interfaces settings here see that real source IP but have no ACL
# concept to filter it. In other words: exposure control belongs ENTIRELY
# at the compose/port-publishing level (which host interface gets mapped),
# not inside this file -- there is no in-container setting that can
# retroactively fix a 0.0.0.0 publish.
listen-address=0.0.0.0
bind-interfaces
port=53

# --- Cache & logging ---------------------------------------------------------
# dnsmasq's own DNS answer cache (unrelated to vault-core's depot cache).
# Default is a low 150 entries -- a household with several game consoles/
# PCs/phones/Steam Decks all repeatedly resolving the same handful of
# steamcontent.com names benefits from a larger cache; 1000 is a generous
# bump at negligible memory cost.
cache-size=1000

# log-queries: log every query dnsmasq answers. In a container this would
# go to stdout/stderr (no log-facility path set, which is what makes
# dnsmasq log there instead of syslog) -- exactly what `docker logs` / the
# json-file logging driver (docs/PROJECT_PLAN.md Docker rules) expects.
#
# PRIVACY NOTE -- why this is OFF by default: unlike vault-core (which only
# ever sees Steam CDN traffic), vault-dns is this LAN's forwarding resolver
# for EVERY domain every client asks it, not just steamcontent.com --
# turning this on logs full browsing-metadata-level query history (every
# hostname every device resolves) to disk/stdout indefinitely. That's a
# meaningfully bigger privacy footprint than "cache debug logging" implies,
# especially for a homelab a household shares, so it defaults OFF here.
# Uncomment the line below only for active debugging (e.g. confirming
# requirement A12's "is my client even asking vault-dns" question), and
# turn it back off again afterward:
#
# log-queries
#
# See dns/README.md's "Privacy note: query logging" section for the full
# reasoning and how to enable it safely for a short debugging session.
